let's speak now if we can to the uh  ambassador or the ukrainian ambassador  to the united kingdom of adam  prischteiko ambassador great to have you  on the program this morning um i just  wonder if we can we can start with the  the military efforts that are continuing  to be made there is an awful lot of  disinformation that is flying around at  this moment in the crisis and and  largely coming from the russian side can  we put a number  on the number of russian soldiers that  have been killed as a result of their at  war with ukraine i've seen the numbers  which our official number is more than  14 000 people already the united states  estimating around  910 russians are actually reducing  obviously to a couple of thousands of  these people dead  how have you been able to achieve that i  mean certainly vladimir putin thought it  would be a simple matter of mobilizing  on the border heading into ukraine where  they would be welcomed as liberators as  heroes clearly that hasn't happened i  believe he's miscalculated a couple of  things first of all that we are not just  starting to fight we've been fighting  with him and with his proxy for eight  years so our forces are sort of battle  hardened at the same time what we also  have that we have just defended our own  land that's what he mistake that he  still believes that we are the same  people we have our lantern we have our  direction to go that's different from  what he has for us  it does fuel however the russian  russian tactics have have changed  significantly in the past few days the  advances that they've made have come to  a halt they appear to be regrouping  particularly in areas like around the  capital and so on but the artillery  bombardment and the far power from the  air continues to smash cities  including but not limited to maripo  you're right they're using ships from  the black sea they're using the uh air  and helicopters from occupied crimea to  get us and most of the rockets just sent  to beautiful cities like here like like  ideas so they are threatening yes right  in big cities like kharkivan cave  so how then do we do we bring an end to  the violence we understand of course  that there have been negotiations taking  place there was a call from your  president to have face-to-face talks  that doesn't appear to happen between  the leaders obviously but but do you see  a solution a negotiated solution to what  is happening in ukraine and if so what  will you have to concede  we believe there are two ways one of the  negotiations which also different  channels and all of them are working the  turks israeli helping us but we also  have our own negotiations on the border  between ukraine and belarus and this is  very technical and very painful  negotiations they've been doing for four  weeks already but what we can do at the  same time we can actually you know  defend ourselves with everything we have  and everything we are receiving from  nations like you and this is helping  that's why we are so effective that's  why we can burn so many tanks because we  have enough enough firepower to stop  these tanks it's not enough to repel  them from our territory yet but we are  working there just on just on the  question of enough fire power the the  reports that have been uh circulating  the past 24 hours suggesting that  actually such has been the tempo of the  ukrainian military activity that you are  beginning to to start to see a need for  more assistance from the west again when  it comes to armaments i mean is that the  case are you running out of weaponry  no not that much we we don't have enough  it didn't have enough in the first place  so running out of weaponry that's that's  we'll be seeing in the in the week to  come  because tomorrow presidency will talk to  nato the whole nato we will see how can  we replenish our stocks and what we can  have much longer range and stronger than  everything here we have enough weapons  to stop tanks immediately when they're  approaching to us but to clear out our  land we have to have something with a  much  greater distance yeah i mean just just  in terms of the the humanitarian cost of  all of this the longer those  bombardments go on the long the greater  the number of civilians innocent  civilians  will be dead as a result of all of this  just give us an idea of what what the  situation is like in in mariopol itself  at the moment we've all seen the  pictures there doesn't appear to be very  much of the city left frankly used to be  four hundred thousand cities very resort  like like brighton on a beach of very  shallow very warm silvers off where  mostly kids would go for the resorts  with their parents now it's more more or  less nothing what we're trying to  achieve we're trying to withdraw as many  civilians as we can unfortunately the  situation is not good the people are  lacking basic foods sometimes even water  but these people are very resilient  they're trying to to to stay where they  are we have troops within the city  they're fighting really furiously and  this is veterans of the eight years were  they know how to fight and they're  putting up a real fight if you see the  picture how russian tanks are burning  just coming through city that's what  what is happening there yeah you i mean  you don't have to to be a military  expert to have been impressed by the  fortitude that has been displayed by the  ukrainian people in the face of a  russian invasion and war at the same  time the strategy of attrition which  vladimir putin has instituted the mass  the wholesale destruction of urban areas  massive civilian casualties you can be  the strongest ukrainian in the world but  after a while that is going to have a  debilitating event on ukrainian morale  surely i understand what he's trying to  achieve he that's his tactic you know to  soften our negotiation position but just  killing our own people and besieging  these cities that this is this barbaric  sort of approach that's something he was  so proud of being from saint peter's  book in leningrad that the city was  besieged for more than two years two  years during world war ii and that's  coming with this proud  lingard  dweller he's doing the same to ukrainian  cities this is something very difficult  to you know to even calculate to  understand  just give us an idea then of of what it  is that ukraine would would like to see  from the west obviously the nato meeting  is pending but particularly as regards  the united kingdom it does feel that the  munitions the skill set that we had  previously exported to ukraine all of  that has proved very very useful indeed  you i don't see anything any limits here  in cooperation with you that that's you  know i'm very proud to be right here in  this capital we have everything we have  weapons we have support political  support we have financial support with  at the time when needed we have been  food being sent even the food the simple  bags and the boxes will be sent to  ukrainians everything we can come with  with refugees every every every aspect  of our war right now on our defense  that's been supported and held by by the  uk  i do want to get your view because  there's been an awful lot of speculation  in this country by britain's rather than  ukrainians about the way in which boris  johnson's word to the conservative  conference at the weekend would have  been interpreted by ordinary ukrainians  i wonder we'll just we'll just play it  first just to remind our viewers exactly  what he said but it was  an allusion to an a to a comparison  let's play  it's the instinct of the people of this  country  like the people of ukraine  to choose freedom every time  when the british people voted for brexit  in such large numbers i don't believe it  because they were remotely hostile to to  foreigners  it's because they wanted to be free  to do things differently and for this  country to be able to run itself  the prime minister there and many people  domestically thought that i was fairly  crass a comparison between you know a  vote to leave a political and economic  union and you and your countrymen  picking up arms to repel a russian  invasion what did you make of what the  prime minister said i believe i was  sitting at the  front row when i was listening to it i  didn't see that this reaction is coming  because what we heard in the room and i  were i heard myself that's actually we  are fighting for our freedom the freedom  to do what the nation wants to do if you  wanted to leave the european union  that's your own decision sovereign  decision we respect it we'd like you to  be in in the european union but we  respect this decision if we wanted to  leave something as a soviet union  we had to have a right and look at the  differences you believe you have  problems with the european union when  you left it's not a problem if you  compare to what we have with russians  when we left the soviet union they came  to kill us for this decision  i wonder what you make of of vladimir  putin at the moment and a number of  people have felt comfortable calling him  a war criminal we've heard from across  the atlantic accusations that he's been  participating in acts of genocide  actually the question i wish to ask you  is what do you think vladimir putin's  mental state is at the moment i believe  that he is delusional now  he was a very reasonable man in his own  way i had the privilege a couple of  times to defend ukrainian positions  sometimes it was with his with his  people and himself personally so i  understand what he thinks he doesn't see  ukrainians as a nation not just what he  was told on many many different  occasions but he just you can see by  conversing with him he despises you he  don't believe that you're on the same  level with him because he's almost sort  of off of russia and everything who are  you talking to him that's the mistake he  made and he made me say for the whole  nation not for the humanity see i i  suppose the question that follows that  is given given your assessment of of the  man the way in which he operates is is  there any way that the violence will  come to an end in ukraine whilst he  remains in the kremlin you know it's  very difficult to believe and so so so  little uh can be said and so a few  people believe but we're actually  winning we can't win with your support  with your assistance we can actually  turn the tide and for this once in a  while we can actually resolve the  situation we don't want to kill russians  or russia itself they will survive what  is happening here this regime might come  to end  define then victory what what does  victory look like because to my mind  russian troops standing five meters the  other side of the ukrainian border that  that doesn't look like victory to me  not to me either i would like to be in  something which would allow us to have  guarantees like like nato that's why  putin is going so against net because he  knows if he miscalculated now he can  come regroup and come later we have to  do something which will allow ukraine to  feel itself safe something we hope to  have after budapest when we signed the  agreement to give out the nuclear  weapons it didn't work so i don't want  to sign any other piece of paper it  won't work either there will be any  number of people watching at this this  morning ambassador who have perhaps  donated food parcels who have perhaps  donated some of their money who are  perhaps even preparing themselves to  open their doors to to your fellow  countrymen and women who maybe will be  arriving here very shortly i wonder if  you've got a message um for the british  people this morning i have a message for  them and i i know that i will be able to  bring this message also in london this  saturday when i will see the crowds of  people with the mayor of london we just  want to thank thank you and you know  people will do it much better than  myself those people who would come here  and feel the worms and in kindness and  response from from the whole uk they  will feel and they will bring this  message what i can tell you that we will  survive and you will have a new friend  on other sides of the of the european  continent we don't have many things in  our history we are starting to build our  new relations  adam bristek great to have you in the  studio this morning thank you so much  for joining us

